From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian McKenna <brian@brianmckenna.org>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 15:48:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(shader): guard loop exits that go through blocks of their
 own

APS5_LOOP_GUARD compared a loop's iteration count with the limit only at
a conditional branch whose target was the loop's merge block. A loop
whose exits first go through a block of their own (a store, a select
feeding the merge block's Phi) counted its iterations on every back edge
and never compared the count: a 837k-word compute shader had four such
loops, exiting on counts read from the title's data, and no compare with
the limit at all, so its module was the same whatever the limit.

Each loop now knows its blocks: those the header dominates that can
still reach a back edge without passing the header, found from the
structured CFG. A conditional branch from one of them to a block outside
them leaves the loop, whatever it targets, and the guard can force it
(a branch that leaves several loops at once checks each of their
counts). This also covers a loop whose only exits are unconditional
branches inside an if. Iterations are counted on the back edges
themselves, and the count is cleared only on entries from outside the
loop, where a back edge from any block but the continue target used to
clear it.

Tested: tests/LoopGuardTests.cpp gains a loop whose two data-dependent
exits each store before the code after the loop. It fails without the
change ("the exits compare the loop's iteration count with the limit")
and passes with it under Wine, built against Rebox's MinGW build of the
recompiler, as does LoopGuardStageTests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
